<p>A month ago, <a href="https://tinkerdwarf.com/">The Tinkering Dwarf</a> wrote that <a href="https://tinkerdwarf.com/writing-is-hard-when-stressed/">writing is hard when stressed</a>. It was a small post, one of those &ldquo;life happens&rdquo; vents, but it resonated with me.</p>
<p>I was just before <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/09/08/almost-time-to-leave-an.html">my vacation to Maine</a>. I haven&rsquo;t been sleeping well, which isn&rsquo;t new, but I&rsquo;ve started connecting a couple of dots with my physical health as well. I gained weight, and for the first time in my life, some of my pants stopped being comfortable. The <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/08/16/rsi-and-emacs-when-your.html">RSI issue I wrote about</a> occurred around the time I had an annoying eye infection. 5 Hours of sleep a night meant even less energy to do anything.</p>
<p>My mental energy is mostly spent on work, which means other areas like writing have been slowing down.</p>
<p>I started a process of correcting this, and in a way I hope that writing it down here would make it more &ldquo;official&rdquo; for me to follow. One unexpected difference between writing for work vs. writing for leisure has been my new keyboard: I use it fully now inside Emacs, but it&rsquo;s still too slow for work, so I leave it for posts and writing in my journal. The quiet that comes with it (it doesn&rsquo;t make typing sounds) and the slowness allow me to concentrate more on my intention writing, which I enjoy.</p>
